---

<StarlightPage
	frontmatter={{
		title: 'Circular investments in AI',
		description: 'A causal loop diagram of the circular investments among the companies that make, rent out and use AI hardware, as reported up to November 2026.',
		part: 'documentation',
		section: 'tools',
		parent: tool,
	}}
	headings={[
		{ depth: 2, slug: 'the-diagram', text: 'The diagram' },
		{ depth: 2, slug: 'the-loops', text: 'The loops' },
		{ depth: 2, slug: 'the-arrangements', text: 'The arrangements' },
		{ depth: 2, slug: 'what-it-leaves-out', text: 'What it leaves out' },
		{ depth: 2, slug: 'the-document', text: 'The document' },
	]}
>
	<p class="adp-lede">
		Chip makers invest in the developers of AI models, the developers commit the money to cloud capacity, and the clouds fill their data centres with the investors' chips. This example draws that circle as a <a href={tool.href}>causal loop diagram</a>: what each company earns, invests, commits and borrows, and how a change in one travels round to the others.
	</p>

	<h2 id="the-diagram">The diagram</h2>
	<p>
		<a href={example.href}>Open the diagram as a flow page</a>: influence moves along the links as dots, and you can push a variable, change a link or follow a loop. It is the VS Code host's export of the document at the foot of this page, and nothing you change in it is kept.
	</p>
	<figure>
		<Image src={picture} alt="The flow page of the diagram: the chip makers along the top, OpenAI on the left and Anthropic on the right of the centre, Oracle and CoreWeave at the far left, Amazon and Google at the far right, and investor confidence, the gap between commitments and end-user revenue and the cost of credit along the bottom, with the named loops listed beside the drawing." widths={[800, 1600]} sizes="(min-width: 50rem) 50rem, 100vw" />
		<figcaption>The flow page as it opens.</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<p>
		Each company is drawn as a few quantities that can rise or fall, such as Nvidia's data-centre revenue and the investments and guarantees it makes, or OpenAI's capital and the compute it commits to. A link with <code>+</code> means the two move together, and one with <code>-</code> means they move apart. Twenty-seven quantities and fifty-six links stand for the arrangements in the table below, together with three quantities outside the circle: what end users pay for AI, the gap between commitments and that revenue, and the cost of credit.
	</p>

	<h2 id="the-loops">The loops</h2>
	<p>
		The links close 153 cycles, of which the document names twenty. Fourteen reinforce a change. R1 to R4 run from Nvidia's revenue through its investments to OpenAI, Anthropic and CoreWeave, and back through their purchases of Nvidia systems, directly or through Oracle's data centres. R5 to R9 are the same circle for the clouds: Microsoft, Amazon and Google invest in a model developer that commits the money to their own capacity. R10 and R11 are AMD's: a warrant whose value rises with AMD's share price, and so with OpenAI's own purchases, and an investment in Anthropic tied to Anthropic's deployment of AMD chips. R12 to R14 run through investor confidence, which sets the price of each new funding round.
	</p>
	<p>
		Six balance. Commitments that run ahead of end-user revenue erode confidence (B1, B2). Borrowing for the build-out makes credit dearer and slows it (B3, B4), as do power and construction delays at the sites (B5). Nvidia's guarantees of its partners' leases add to the cost of credit, which weighs on the confidence its own customers raise money on (B6).
	</p>
	<p>The only money entering the circle from outside it is what end users pay for AI: in the flow page it is the one variable with an outside push. Every reinforcing loop speeds up a rise and a fall alike.</p>

	<h2 id="the-arrangements">The arrangements</h2>
	<TableScroll label="The arrangements">
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th scope="col">Parties</th>
					<th scope="col">What was agreed</th>
					<th scope="col">Links in the diagram</th>
					<th scope="col">Reported by</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				{
					arrangements.map((arrangement) => (
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">{arrangement.parties}</th>
							<td>{arrangement.terms}</td>
							<td>{arrangement.link}</td>
							<td>
								{arrangement.sources.map((source, index) => (
									<>
										{index > 0 && '; '}
										{source.publisher}, <a href={source.href}><cite>{source.title}</cite></a>, {source.date}
									</>
								))}
							</td>
						</tr>
					))
				}
			</tbody>
		</table>
	</TableScroll>

	<h2 id="what-it-leaves-out">What it leaves out</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Sizes. A link says which way a change moves its effect, not how much: a $300 billion contract and a $2 billion stake are one link each.</li>
		<li>Status. Letters of intent, term sheets, conditional tranches and completed investments are drawn alike; the table says which each is.</li>
		<li>Other companies and arrangements, among them xAI, Meta, SoftBank, the neoclouds other than CoreWeave and the private credit funds behind many data centres.</li>
		<li>Time. The arrangements are as reported up to 25 November 2026, and several have changed since they were first announced.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>The numbers on the flow page come from a simple model of the diagram's structure. They are not measurements or forecasts.</p>

	<h2 id="the-document">The document</h2>
	<p>The diagram is this <code>.cld</code> document. The positions of its variables are kept beside it, in the registration a host reads.</p>
	<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="ai-circular-investments.cld"><code>{document}</code></pre>
</StarlightPage>
